>

```
<div class="container">

    <p class="eyebrow">
        FIRST PRINCIPLE
    </p>

    <h2>
        What Is Hatha Yoga?
    </h2>

    <p>
        Hatha Yoga is a major stream of the broader Yoga
        tradition that gives an important place to the body,
        breath, concentration and inner practice.
    </p>

    <p>
        Classical Hatha Yoga texts present physical and
        breathing practices as part of a larger path.
    </p>

    <p>
        It is therefore too narrow to describe Hatha Yoga
        simply as a collection of physical postures.
    </p>

    <p>
        In the classical literature, practices involving
        asana, pranayama, purification, mudra, bandha,
        meditation and samadhi are connected to a deeper
        transformation of the practitioner.
    </p>

    <p>
        Modern Yoga uses the term Hatha in many different ways.
        This website will distinguish modern classroom usage
        from historical Hatha Yoga traditions.
    </p>

</div>
```

</section>

<!-- =====================================================
     HA AND THA
===================================================== -->

<section class="content-section alt-section">

```
<div class="container">

    <p class="eyebrow">
        A CAREFUL READING
    </p>

    <h2>
        What Does “Haṭha” Mean?
    </h2>

    <p>
        The word Haṭha has been interpreted in different ways
        within Yoga traditions and later explanations.
    </p>

    <p>
        A popular traditional interpretation connects
        “ha” with the sun and “ṭha” with the moon.
    </p>

    <p>
        This symbolism is associated with balancing and
        integrating complementary forces.
    </p>

    <p>
        It is important, however, not to reduce the entire
        history of Hatha Yoga to one modern slogan.
    </p>

    <p>
        Classical Hatha texts contain a much richer vocabulary
        involving prana, nadis, kundalini, mudras, bandhas,
        meditation and samadhi.
    </p>

</div>
```

</section>

