<section class="content-section alt-section">

```
<div class="container">

    <p class="eyebrow">
        PRANA
    </p>

    <h2>
        Prana in the Yoga Tradition
    </h2>

    <p>
        Prana is an important concept in Indian Yoga traditions.
    </p>

    <p>
        It is often translated as life force or vital energy,
        but the traditional concept does not map perfectly onto
        a single modern scientific term.
    </p>

    <p>
        Hatha Yoga uses the language of prana to explain
        relationships between breath, vitality, attention
        and subtle practice.
    </p>

    <div class="notice">

        <h3>
            Traditional Concept and Modern Science
        </h3>

        <p>
            On this website, traditional descriptions of prana
            will be presented as traditional Yoga concepts.
        </p>

        <p>
            They will not automatically be presented as
            scientifically established anatomical facts.
        </p>

    </div>

</div>
```

</section>

<!-- =====================================================
     NADIS
===================================================== -->

<section class="content-section">

```
<div class="container">

    <p class="eyebrow">
        SUBTLE BODY
    </p>

    <h2>
        Nadis and the Subtle Body
    </h2>

    <p>
        Hatha Yoga literature describes a network of subtle
        channels called nadis.
    </p>

    <p>
        Among the best-known are Ida, Pingala and Sushumna.
    </p>

    <div class="card-grid">

        <article class="card">

            <h3>
                Ida
            </h3>

            <p>
                Traditionally described as one of the major
                subtle channels.
            </p>

        </article>


        <article class="card">

            <h3>
                Pingala
            </h3>

            <p>
                Traditionally described as another major
                subtle channel.
            </p>

        </article>


        <article class="card">

            <h3>
                Sushumna
            </h3>

            <p>
                Traditionally described as the central channel
                associated with advanced inner practice.
            </p>

        </article>

    </div>

    <p>
        These belong to the traditional subtle-body model.
        They should not be confused with nerves, blood vessels
        or other structures identified by modern anatomy.
    </p>

</div>
```

</section>

