<!-- =====================================================
     FOUR CLASSICAL HATHA TEXTS
===================================================== -->

<section class="content-section alt-section">

```
<div class="container">

    <p class="eyebrow">
        CLASSICAL TEXTUAL HERITAGE
    </p>

    <h2>
        Three Major Classical Hatha Yoga Texts
    </h2>

    <p>
        Three texts are especially important when studying
        the classical Hatha Yoga literature.
    </p>

    <div class="card-grid">

        <article class="card">

            <h3>
                Haṭha Yoga Pradīpikā
            </h3>

            <p>
                Traditionally attributed to Svātmārāma.
            </p>

            <p>
                Its four chapters address asana, pranayama
                and purification, mudra and samadhi.
            </p>

        </article>


        <article class="card">

            <h3>
                Śiva Saṃhitā
            </h3>

            <p>
                A major Hatha Yoga text with discussions of
                Yoga, philosophy, subtle practices, asana,
                mudra and the guru-student relationship.
            </p>

        </article>


        <article class="card">

            <h3>
                Gheraṇḍa Saṃhitā
            </h3>

            <p>
                A later classical text organized around a
                seven-limbed system of practice.
            </p>

        </article>

    </div>

    <p>
        These texts should be studied as historical and
        philosophical works rather than treated as identical
        instruction manuals.
    </p>

</div>
```

</section>

<!-- =====================================================
     HATHA YOGA PRADIPIKA
===================================================== -->

<section class="content-section">

```
<div class="container">

    <p class="eyebrow">
        SVĀTMĀRĀMA'S TEXT
    </p>

    <h2>
        The Haṭha Yoga Pradīpikā
    </h2>

    <p>
        The Haṭha Yoga Pradīpikā is one of the most influential
        surviving classical Hatha Yoga manuals.
    </p>

    <p>
        It contains four major chapters and presents a progression
        from physical practice toward subtler inner practice.
    </p>

    <div class="card-grid">

        <article class="card">

            <h3>
                Chapter One
            </h3>

            <p>
                Asana and foundations for practice.
            </p>

        </article>


        <article class="card">

            <h3>
                Chapter Two
            </h3>

            <p>
                Shatkarma and pranayama.
            </p>

        </article>


        <article class="card">

            <h3>
                Chapter Three
            </h3>

            <p>
                Mudras and bandhas.
            </p>

        </article>


        <article class="card">

            <h3>
                Chapter Four
            </h3>

            <p>
                Samadhi and inner sound practice.
            </p>

        </article>

    </div>

    <p>
        The text therefore presents Hatha Yoga as considerably
        more than physical exercise.
    </p>

</div>
```

</section>

<!-- =====================================================
     GHERAṆḌA SYSTEM
===================================================== -->

<section class="content-section alt-section">

```
<div class="container">

    <p class="eyebrow">
        SEVEN-LIMBED HATHA YOGA
    </p>

    <h2>
        The Gheraṇḍa Saṃhitā Approach
    </h2>

    <p>
        The Gheraṇḍa Saṃhitā presents a sevenfold system.
    </p>

    <div class="card-grid">

        <article class="card">
            <h3>1. Ṣaṭkarma</h3>
            <p>Purification practices.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
            <h3>2. Āsana</h3>
            <p>Physical steadiness and conditioning.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
            <h3>3. Mudrā</h3>
            <p>Traditional seals and practices.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
            <h3>4. Pratyāhāra</h3>
            <p>Withdrawal and regulation of the senses.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
            <h3>5. Prāṇāyāma</h3>
            <p>Breath and prana practices.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
            <h3>6. Dhyāna</h3>
            <p>Meditative practice.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
            <h3>7. Samādhi</h3>
            <p>Deep meditative absorption.</p>
        </article>

    </div>

    <p>
        This seven-limbed arrangement is different from
        Patanjali's eight-limbed framework. Both should be
        studied in their own historical contexts.
    </p>

</div>
```

</section>

